<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit receiver (ADR-0062, .github#1262). This repo is a `coordination-kit` receiver: it holds
    the shared coordination skills and the `fsgg-coord` client on disk. It used to take them by
    byte-copy sync PR (`coordination-propagate`); it now REFERENCES the versioned `FS.GG.Kit` package
    and MATERIALIZES that kit onto disk from its pin. (It also holds the engine tool manifest the shim
    execs, `.config/dotnet-tools.json` — but that is OURS, not the kit's, since 0.18.0; see below.)
    A hub change reaches this repo
    as an ordinary Renovate bump of the pin below, and `kit-materialize.yml` re-runs the materialize on
    that PR — the same shape lockfile-sync uses for packages.lock.json.

    This project exists ONLY to reference the package and drive its materialize. It compiles nothing and
    is deliberately NOT part of FS.GG.Templates.csproj, so the normal build never touches it. Materialize:

        dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize

    The materialized files (skills, scripts/fsgg-coord) are COMMITTED: the materialize is the write arm
    that replaces the byte-copy, not a live per-build input, so it does NOT run on every build
    (FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild=false). The committed bytes are content-addressed and byte-identical to
    canonical, so `coordination-coherence` stays green.

    `.config/dotnet-tools.json` IS NOT AMONG THEM AND HAS NOT BEEN SINCE FS.GG.Kit 0.18.0
    (FS-GG/.github#1615, ADR-0068). It is THIS REPO'S OWN FILE now: the kit's `kit:` block no longer
    carries `dist/dotnet/.config/dotnet-tools.json`, the materialize never writes our copy, and
    `coordination-coherence` no longer grades it — kit members went 9 -> 8. Nothing was lost in the
    handover, because the file was already tracked here; the kit simply stopped overwriting it. Do not
    re-add it to the list above on the theory that a kit receiver must hold a kit-shaped manifest.

    WHY IT LEFT THE KIT. The manifest pins the `fs.gg.coord.cli` engine, so an engine version bump was
    editing HASHED kit content — it staled `registry/repos.lock`, reddened `kit-published-coherence`,
    and obliged a full kit republish plus a seven-receiver fan-out to move one integer in one JSON file.

    THE ENGINE BUMP IS NOT YET LIVE HERE, so do not read the paragraph above as "Renovate has it".
    ADR-0068's plan is that Renovate's nuget manager bumps `fs.gg.coord.cli` in each receiver directly
    (`/(^|/)dotnet-tools\.json$/` is one of its shipped `managerFilePatterns`), but FS-GG/.github's
    `default.json` still carries a `packageRules` entry matching `.config/dotnet-tools.json` with
    `enabled: false` — written when the file WAS kit-materialized and a bump here would have been
    guaranteed-red drift. Until that rule is lifted the delivery path is filed, not working:
    FS-GG/.github#1798. Bump this file by hand meanwhile.

    WHAT KEEPS US HONEST NOW IS CENTRAL, NOT LOCAL. FS-GG/.github's `repos-audit` engine-manifest sweep
    (#1615, daily at 08:17 UTC) asserts against every `receives: coordination-kit` repo's ACTUAL TREE
    that it declares `fs.gg.coord.cli` with a usable version — "a repo that receives the `fsgg-coord`
    shim can actually run the engine the shim execs". So deleting this file, or dropping the tool entry
    out of it, is caught centrally within a day rather than silently. That is strictly stronger than
    what it replaced: the old guarantee was `f(roster)` — the shim and the manifest were both `kit:`
    rows, so their receiver sets were equal BY CONSTRUCTION — which was blind in exactly the direction
    that matters, since a receiver that deleted its own copy by hand stayed green forever because
    nothing ever read its tree. The sweep does NOT grade the engine VERSION; "can run the engine at
    all" is the invariant, not "runs the newest one".

    AND THIS REPO IS PRECISELY THE CASE THAT SWEEP WAS WRITTEN FOR. FS.GG.Templates and FS.GG.Audio
    were the TWO repos FS-GG/.github#1077 originally caught holding the `fsgg-coord` shim with NO
    ENGINE TO EXEC. The manifest used to ride `build-config`, and we deliberately do NOT
    `receive: build-config` (see below) — so the shim reached us and the engine did not. #1077 fixed
    that by making the manifest a `coordination-kit` row; #1615 unmade the row, which is why the
    invariant had to be re-established as a real assertion over our tree. The sweep's subject is every
    kit receiver rather than every *package* receiver for exactly this reason: narrowing it would carve
    #1077's two original victims back out of the check written to replace it. If a future change here
    makes this file disappear, #1077 recurs in this repo first.

    ONE RUNTIME ROOT IS NO LONGER AMONG THE MATERIALIZED FILES EITHER (FS-GG/.github#1676, ADR-0067 §9
    phase 4, 2026-07-28), for an unrelated reason — this one is about skills, not the manifest. The
    skills are materialized and committed at `.claude/skills` ONLY. `.agents/skills` is a VIEW root: it
    is still in the runtime contract and both runtimes still load it, but it is git-ignored and
    RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout instead of being a second committed copy of the same
    bytes. See the FsggKitViewSkillRoots block below, and FsggTemplatesGenerateSkillView at the bottom
    of this file. `coordination-coherence` narrows with it — from 51 graded files to 28, measured — and
    stays green, because it derives its roots from FsggKitSkillRoots.

    This repo does NOT `receive: build-config`, so FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig stays off (its default).
    Unlike the other receivers it does NOT use Central Package Management (no Directory.Packages.props),
    so the FS.GG.Kit version is pinned DIRECTLY on the PackageReference below — Renovate updates it there.
    FS.GG.Kit is restored anonymously from nuget.org (the repo has no nuget.config, so the default source
    applies); the package is dual-published there (ADR-0062), so no credential is needed.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A TFM is required by the SDK; nothing is compiled. net10.0 is the org band; the SDK is the one
         setup-dotnet installs in kit-materialize.yml (10.0.x) — this repo pins no global.json. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Tooling-only: no source, no output, no lockfile of its own. -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>false</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <!-- No CPM in this repo, so do not let an inherited/implicit central-management setting reject the
         version-bearing PackageReference below (NU1008). -->
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>false</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>

    <!-- ADR-0067 §9 phase 4, first receiver (FS-GG/.github#1676). `.agents/skills` is a VIEW root now,
         not a materialized one: still in the runtime contract, still loaded by both runtimes, but its
         content is RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout by `scripts/skill-view generate` instead
         of being a second committed copy of it. The RUNTIME ROOT SET IS UNCHANGED — ADR-0065 §A root's
         three dispositions makes it the UNION of these two properties, and that union is still
         ADR-0011's two roots. Nothing is retired here; one copy stopped existing.

         WHY `.claude/skills` IS THE LIVE ROOT AND NOT THE VIEW. The kit's four skill rows source
         `.claude/skills/<id>` in FS-GG/.github `registry/repos.yml`, and `registry/repos.lock`
         content-addresses those exact paths — so the materialize writes there, and
         `coordination-coherence` grades those bytes against this repo's pin. Making `.agents/skills`
         the live root instead would rename a `kit:` source, which obliges a kit republish and a
         seven-receiver fan-out for a path rename. See FS-GG/.github
         `docs/coordination/skill-apparatus-retirement-order.md` §3. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots>.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>

    <!-- Materialize into the repo ROOT (two levels up from .config/kit/), on demand — never on build. -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../'))</FsggKitReceiverRoot>
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>false</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version pinned directly (no CPM). Renovate-managed. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FS.GG.Kit" Version="0.58.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE VIEW IS GENERATED BEFORE IT IS ASSERTED, IN EVERY TREE THE MATERIALIZE RUNS IN.

    `FsggKitCheckSkillView` (ADR-0067 §8, AfterTargets=FsggKitMaterialize) fails the build when a view
    root is absent, dangling, or partly visible — deliberately, because all three of those are exit 0
    with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes, and "a rewrite that removes the loud failure and adds the quiet
    one is worse than no rewrite." A view root is NOT committed (a committed symlink degrades to a plain
    text file under `git -c core.symlinks=false`, ADR-0067 §6), so EVERY fresh checkout starts without
    one: a clean clone, a new worktree, and `kit-materialize.yml`'s runner alike. Measured on a fresh
    clone of this repo at the retirement commit — without this target the materialize FAILS with
    "view skill root '.agents/skills' is ABSENT or a DANGLING link".

    So the generate belongs next to the assertion, not in a workflow step that only some of those trees
    run. The tool is `scripts/skill-view` — THIS repo's copy, delivered and byte-verified by the
    FS.GG.Kit version this repo pins (a `kind: client` kit row since 0.15.0), never one fetched from a
    moving hub `main`: a receiver's verdict must be a function of the receiver (FS-GG/.github#1584).

    The `roots` flag names the view roots EXPLICITLY rather than letting the tool fall back to
    ADR-0011's default pair. The fallback would also try to resolve `.claude/skills`, this repo's
    TRACKED live root, and `skill-view` refuses to generate over a tracked root with no override, so it
    would be a refusal rather than a no-op.

    NOT conditioned on the OS. `skill-view` is bash; there is no Windows CI here, and a Windows tree
    without bash SHOULD fail loudly rather than quietly materialize a kit whose second runtime root is
    empty. The `Condition` guards on the PROPERTY, so if a later change empties FsggKitViewSkillRoots
    this target stops running with it instead of generating a view nothing asserts.
  -->
  <Target Name="FsggTemplatesGenerateSkillView" BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"
          Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' != ''">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)"
          Command="bash scripts/skill-view generate --source $(FsggKitSkillRoots) --roots &quot;$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots.Replace(';',' '))&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
